  <article class="max-w-3xl">
    <h1 class="text-3xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How Many Recessed Lights Do I Need for a 2-Car Garage?</h1>
    <div class="text-xs text-gray-500 mb-6">Published on July 12, 2026 • 5 min read</div>
    
    <div class="prose prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 space-y-5">
      <p class="text-lg">
        Upgrading an outdated, single-bulb garage ceiling fixture to flush ultra-thin LED recessed lights (canless wafer lights) is one of the highest-impact DIY home improvements you can make. A bright, evenly lit garage transforms dark storage into a clean, safe workspace for woodworking, detailing cars, or organizing tools.
      </p>
      
      <p>
        However, garages present unique lighting challenges. Unlike living rooms with soft ambient requirements, garages need intense task lighting without creating blind spots around open car doors or overhead garage door tracks. Getting your fixture count and grid layout right prevents dim corners and harsh glares.
      </p>

      <!-- Calculator CTA -->
      <div class="my-8 p-6 bg-gradient-to-br from-blue-50 to-blue-100 border border-blue-200 rounded-xl shadow-sm">
        <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-blue-900 mb-2 mt-0">Skip the Math: Use Our Free Garage Calculator</h2>
        <p class="text-blue-800 mb-4">Want an exact layout grid for your specific garage dimensions? Enter your length, width, and ceiling height into our free tool to generate a precise fixture count and spacing scheme.</p>
        <a href="/calculators/recessed-lights-garage" class="inline-block bg-blue-600 hover:bg-blue-700 text-white font-semibold py-3 px-6 rounded-lg transition-colors">
          Go to Garage Recessed Light Calculator →
        </a>
      </div>

      <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Step-by-Step: How to Calculate Garage Can Light Count</h2>
      
      <p>Follow these four straightforward steps used by electrical contractors to design a bright, shadow-free garage ceiling grid.</p>

      <div class="space-y-6 mt-6">
        <div>
          <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 flex items-center gap-2">
            <span class="bg-blue-100 text-blue-700 rounded-full w-6 h-6 flex items-center justify-center text-xs shrink-0">1</span>
            Determine Total Square Footage
          </h3>
          <p class="mt-2">Measure the length and width of your garage floor in feet. A standard 2-car garage typically ranges from <strong>20x20 ft (400 sq ft)</strong> to <strong>24x24 ft (576 sq ft)</strong>. Multiply length by width to get your total area.</p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 flex items-center gap-2">
            <span class="bg-blue-100 text-blue-700 rounded-full w-6 h-6 flex items-center justify-center text-xs shrink-0">2</span>
            Calculate Target Lumens Based on Use
          </h3>
          <p class="mt-2">Multiply your square footage by your desired foot-candles (lumens per sq ft):</p>
          <ul class="list-disc pl-5 mt-2 space-y-1 text-sm">
            <li><strong>Standard Parking &amp; General Storage (50 Lumens/sq ft):</strong> 400 sq ft × 50 = <strong>20,000 total lumens</strong>.</li>
            <li><strong>Workshop &amp; Auto Detailing (75 Lumens/sq ft):</strong> 576 sq ft × 75 = <strong>43,200 total lumens</strong>.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <div>
          <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 flex items-center gap-2">
            <span class="bg-blue-100 text-blue-700 rounded-full w-6 h-6 flex items-center justify-center text-xs shrink-0">3</span>
            Divide by Fixture Lumen Output
          </h3>
          <p class="mt-2">Standard 6-inch LED wafer lights output approximately 1,000 to 1,200 lumens each. Divide your target lumens by bulb output:</p>
          <p class="bg-gray-50 p-3 rounded border text-sm font-mono mt-2">20,000 Lumens / 1,100 Lumens per light = 18.1 → Round to 16 or 20 for balanced grid symmetry.</p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 flex items-center gap-2">
            <span class="bg-blue-100 text-blue-700 rounded-full w-6 h-6 flex items-center justify-center text-xs shrink-0">4</span>
            Layout Grid Spacing (The 1/2 Wall Rule)
          </h3>
          <p class="mt-2">To prevent perimeter shadows near workbench counters and garage door tracks:</p>
          <ul class="list-disc pl-5 mt-2 space-y-1 text-sm">
            <li><strong>Wall-to-Light Distance:</strong> Set your outer lights 2 to 3 feet from side walls (half the light-to-light distance).</li>
            <li><strong>Light-to-Light Distance:</strong> Space lights 4 to 6 feet apart across rows and columns.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>

      <hr class="my-10 border-gray-200" />

      <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-gray-900 mb-6">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
      
      <div class="space-y-6">
        <div>
          <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900">How many lumens do I need for a 2-car garage?</h3>
          <p class="mt-1 text-gray-600">For basic vehicle parking and general storage, a 2-car garage (approx. 400 to 576 sq ft) requires 20,000 to 25,000 total lumens (50 lumens per sq ft). If you use the garage as a workshop, detail vehicles, or work with power tools, increase brightness to 30,000–40,000 total lumens (75 lumens per sq ft).</p>
        </div>
        
        <div>
          <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900">How far apart should recessed lights be in a garage?</h3>
          <p class="mt-1 text-gray-600">Space recessed lights approximately 4 to 6 feet apart from each other. Keep perimeter lights 2 to 3 feet away from walls so cabinet tops and garage door tracks don't cast dark shadows.</p>
        </div>
        
        <div>
          <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900">What color temperature light is best for a garage?</h3>
          <p class="mt-1 text-gray-600">4000K (Cool White) or 5000K (Daylight White) is recommended for garages. Daylight temperatures reduce eye strain, make small tool markings legible, and eliminate yellow shadows associated with warm 2700K living room bulbs.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
